    name: null,
    body: "## Highlights\n\n- Temporary provider-capacity refusals no longer trip the hourly automated-start circuit. Fleet Manager keeps dispatching through short OpenAI capacity pressure, while each fresh delegated or scheduled start still observes the provider's one-minute retry floor before trying again ([#4985](https://github.com/tale-project/tale/pull/4985), [#4945](https://github.com/tale-project/tale/issues/4945)). Real automated starts continue to count toward the circuit, so repeated task failures still receive the existing protection.\n\n## Upgrade notes\n\n- From v0.5.90 to v0.5.91 no database migration or configuration change is needed, and the API contract stays at `3.44.0`. The fleet scheduler adopts the retry behavior on its next run after the backend is upgraded; no manual task or schedule changes are required.\n\n## API contract changes\n\nNone in this range. The contract stays at 3.44.0: 161 operations.\n\n\n## What's Changed\n* fix(platform): log why an agent's metadata document fetch failed by @larryro in https://github.com/tale-project/tale/pull/4982\n* feat(sandbox): run sessions on Incus by @yannickmonney in https://github.com/tale-project/tale/pull/4814\n* feat(cli): read a deployment spec written as YAML by @yannickmonney in https://github.com/tale-project/tale/pull/4983\n* fix(platform): keep capacity refusals out of task circuit by @yannickmonney in https://github.com/tale-project/tale/pull/4985\n* docs: prepare Tale v0.5.91 notes by @yannickmonney in https://github.com/tale-project/tale/pull/4986\n\n\n**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/tale-project/tale/compare/v0.5.90...v0.5.91",
